      "chapterNumber": 2,
      "headline": "Creating Value in Negotiation: Bake a Bigger Pie Before Slicing",
      "keyInsight": "Amateurs look at negotiation like two hungry diners bickering over a single pizza slice. If one person eats more cheese, the other person supposedly starves. But real life rarely serves a single pizza topping. One person loves savory pepperoni while the other just wants the garlic dough crust. When you haggle only over cold cash, you turn a rich garden into barren concrete. The hidden gold lies in trading things that cost you pennies but save the other party gold bullion. Think of payment terms, delivery schedules, warranty windows, and future promises. When you group multiple items together into neat combo packages, you stop tugging on a single rope. You transform a bitter tug-of-war into a cooperative kitchen banquet where everybody leaves stuffed.",
      "content": "Picture two neighbors planning a shared backyard fence remodel over coffee. If they argue only over who pays how much cash, tempers flare fast. One feels shortchanged while the other feels ripped off. But home improvement projects come packed with hidden trade-offs. One neighbor happens to own a professional power saw, while the other gets commercial wholesale discounts on cedar lumber.\n\nThe moment they bundle tools, lumber, and weekend labor together, the stubborn dispute evaporates. Each person contributes what is easy for them and receives what is hard to get alone. Smart value creators never settle single issues one by one in a rigid checklist. Single-issue debates turn into petty arm-wrestling matches where someone must surrender.\n\nInstead, place several complete packages on the kitchen table at once. Ask the other side which recipe tastes better to them. Even if they reject all choices, their reaction reveals their hidden dietary cravings. Differences in risk tolerance, project timing, and practical needs are not annoying roadblocks. Differences are the secret spices that allow you to bake a much larger pie together.",
