      "id": "negotiation-genius-ch-5",
      "order": 5,
      "chapterNumber": 5,
      "headline": "Heart Trouble: When Pride And Tempers Burn Down The House",
      "keyInsight": "Calculators never throw tantrums, but human beings certainly do. When a negotiation gets tense, logic packs its bags and leaps out the window. The heart takes over, driven by wounded pride, jealousy, and stubborn spite. Have you ever watched a bidder at an estate auction pay double for an old oak desk simply because another bidder refused to back down? That is escalation of commitment in full bloom. You stop caring about the actual desk. Your sole mission becomes destroying the rival across the room. We throw good money after bad simply to protect our fragile egos from feeling defeated. Worse yet, our sense of fairness is wildly crooked. We demand absolute fairness for ourselves, yet we happily accept an unfair advantage when it falls in our lap. When an offer feels insulting, our emotional engine demands revenge, even if walking away empties our own wallet. True negotiation genius is not about being cold-blooded. It is about realizing that your emotions are expensive luxury items you cannot afford to indulge in the middle of a transaction.",
      "content": "Picture someone tending a garden who discovers a patch of invasive weeds. Instead of pulling them out gently, they grab a blowtorch and scorch the entire vegetable patch in a fit of rage. That is what runaway emotion does to a deal. When someone insults our opening offer, our pulse spikes. We want to punish them more than we want to reach a profitable agreement.\n\nConsider the trap of auction fever. Two buyers enter a bidding contest for a vintage wristwatch. Each bid is no longer about the timepiece itself. It becomes a personal duel to prove who possesses deeper pockets and tougher grit. The winner walks away with the watch, but pays a ruinous price that leaves them with buyer remorse. They won the contest, but lost the war.\n\nEgo also turns negotiations into courtroom battles over who is more virtuous. We view our own concessions as noble sacrifices, while treating the other person's concessions as cheap tricks. If someone offers you ninety dollars out of a free hundred, your heart might tell you to reject it out of pure spite because it is not an even fifty-fifty split. Walking away with zero dollars to punish a stranger is pure heart-driven folly.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Never pay extra money just to punish an opponent or soothe your bruised pride.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Hot emotions like pride and stubborn spite turn profitable deals into expensive personal duels. Taming your ego keeps you focused on real value instead of winning petty point battles.",
